Z||000004||GT Bastard||03-16-2000||09:12 PM||||If V8 power is what you want, be prepared to shell out some money.  <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/</A> <P>There's been quite a few good posts on v6 upgrades, but it's best to just click on the search option and look at previous posts.  later man..||||reg||1
Z||000005||87SC/GT||03-16-2000||09:32 PM||||Phillip, Look at the exhaust that comes factory on the "donor car" That would be a good place to start. There is such a thing as too much flow. There is a member on the forum with a very fast and nearly stock V6 and he is running a single exhaust. While there is no such thing as a "one size fits all" exhaust system, there are always happy mediums. I have been looking at every fiero swap know to man. While I am not an expert by a long shot, I can tell you where to look and  share what I have learned . Contact me by email. Peace.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||03-17-2000||08:31 AM||||If your willing to fork out the BIG money and find someone to set up TRUE dual exhaust 2.25ID pipe will support 350hp. Thats what Flowmaster used on thier "stock replacement" kit for a Moosestang and thats what is't rated for. I've got 2.5" off the stock Y-pipe and a single out. After putting it on the Dyno we noticed a HP and Torque gain with the single exhaust and no gain with the Ocelot upgrade. Flowmaster does make a muffer that will handel the strange configuration needed for our V6 cars, but it's pretty heavy. tips the scales at nearly 30lbs. Its the unit used in the 93-98 Z-28s. it will cram in the area we have but you'll have just paid more money for no real gain in performance. I realized that Dual tips looks better and such. but my own personal experiance, and the numbers of Firehawk, and SLP (High end GM tuners makers of the Firehawk and the Z28-SS) All use the same set up I do (or I copied them, you pick) Single in BIG single out just as BIG. Single out keeps The back pressure the car needs and is able to flow just fine in the higher RPM ranges. a single 2.5" pipe can EASILY support 300HP... WAY more than your 2.8 will push...  And Like 87SC said their is no perfect for everone exhaust.. just whats perfect for your application and performance needs. If you have too large of an exhaust you WILL burn a valve; too small and your car will choke and spit and sputter and generally be crappy. My 2.5" set-up supports this. With No problem, no Noise ordinance problems and you can carry on a conversation in the car with out screaming...  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A>   <BR>If you've got the cash the turbo set up is great. the IHI unit could be upgraded to a HKS T03-04 hybrid unit. smaller exhaust wheel makes it spool up faster and larger compressor wheel means.. you guessed it more CFM. Trying to run 15PSI is nice but if you don't have the air to support it you might as well have 6. The less your turbo has to work the longer it will last and the better the performance will be. And be sure if you do a turbo set up you don't already have really high compression... The higher the compression the less boost you'll be able to run safely with out detonation and cooking a piston... and Make sure you let it cool down after EVER drive. Or buy a Turbo Timer... you'll kill the turbo and not even know it. They get REALLY hot and will cook the oil in there gumming up the bearings and make you spend another fortune fixing a what you thought was a new turbo. Their only $90 and worth EVERY penny. (They let your car Idle down for up to 9 minutes letting the oil circulate and cool the turbo without you being in the car.. and it disables the cars ability to move, so it can't be stolen while running on this unit)  Sorry for the book. Happy speeding!  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||03-17-2000||10:58 AM||||OK. Now that we are talking about mufflers something comes to my mind. We have been talking a lot in other threads about the Borla system and how good it is. Well, it is a muffler with some piping bent specifically for the Fiero. Does anyone knows if Borla still sells only the muffler? That way we may be able to buy it and probably get a muffler shop to add the piping or adapt the piping we have (cut & paste?)on our current muffler to it. Could this be possible? Could be problems due to metal material differences? This is only asuming we can buy a single Borla muffler.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierosound||03-17-2000||11:26 AM||||Borla has mufflers available, just not any to fit the Fiero as far as I can see (I checked).  Their listing may not be complete. You may want to email them and ask.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.borla.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.borla.com/</A> <P>PS - guys - please try to include the link in YOUR reply.  You can also delete your extra posts by selecting the EDIT MESSAGE icon, and checking the DELETE post message at the top of the edit screen.  Thanks.<P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000010||fastonpumpgas||03-17-2000||05:57 PM||||i have done amny desk top gynos and alot of research, in y opinion the ignition and the exhaust suck on a feiro.  i feel that an upgraded 2.8 you could get away with just porting the stock manifolds and see 10 more hp, but if you want to make 225hp or more you have to go with headers and costom daul exhaust....  oh yeah, I wouldnt worry about over doing italthough 2.25 is more than you will ever need on a 2.8||||reg||1
